# Build output
build

# Debug artifacts (GDB scripts, captured stderr, sanity/trace dumps)
*.gdb
*.txt
# ...but not the corpora, which are inputs rather than output. `*.txt` had
# quietly kept tools/limits/real_world.txt - half of what WP-14 measures the
# limit defaults against - out of every clone but this one, so `make
# check-limits` elsewhere would have produced a different table from the one
# documentation/dialects.md section 7 publishes.
!tools/limits/*.txt
# The same trap, caught the same way: `tests/data/vectors/known-gaps.txt` is
# what the conformance runner reads to tell a named gap from a regression, so
# a clone without it would fail 261 vectors that this one passes.
!tests/data/vectors/*.txt
# And its self-test twin, for the same reason one layer down: the self-test
# corpus is what proves the runner can fail a record at all, and since the
# last real gap closed it is the only reader the `gap` category has. Left
# ignored, a fresh clone would carry the deliberately-wrong records in
# `gaps.rxt` with nothing listing them, so the file that exists to make the
# runner go red would make it go red for the wrong reason.
!tests/data/vectors_selftest/*.txt

# IDE / CTest run output (not used by project Makefile)
Testing

# Third-party / reference source trees (not tracked by name)
third_party

# Documentation generated by Doxygen
docs

# Example output files
example.output.*

# Task documents
tasks

# Test output (e.g. PNGs written by encode tests for verification)
tests/out

# Stray output under test data (use tests/out instead)
tests/data/out


# Pycache files (for generated test fixtures, in any directory)
**/__pycache__

# libFuzzer writes its artifacts beside the binary's working directory, which
# is the repository root when a harness is run by hand. A crash or a slow
# unit is a finding to act on, not a file to commit.
/crash-*
/leak-*
/oom-*
/slow-unit-*
/timeout-*
